    "narrative": "As we enter the final stretch of the season, our focus remains laser-focused on efficiency given our limited game caps. **Brice Turang** earns the start at 2B today with a 46.03 score. While Pivetta presents a formidable hurdle with a 3.17 xERA, the shift to the #4 spot in the order provides a vital boost to **Brice Turang**'s run-production upside. Although the SP skill dampens his ceiling by 19%, his elite sprint speed keeps him in the lineup despite the challenging matchup; in a scarcity-driven market, his ability to manufacture stolen bases outweighs the high probability of a sub-optimal batting average result against a pitcher of Pivetta's caliber.\n\nThe bench remains heavy today due to off-days for core contributors like **Corbin Carroll**, **Josh Naylor**, and **James Wood**, along with the continued injury absence of **Tyler Soderstrom**. Among those active but not starting, **Jose Caballero** was the closest to cracking the lineup. While **Jose Caballero** possesses the requisite speed to impact our SB category, his projected output failed to clear the 40-point \"marginal\" threshold against his current opponent, making him a net-negative play when factoring in the preservation of our remaining game caps. We are proceeding with caution as we monitor the lineup status for **Mickey Moniak**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Seiya Suzuki**, **Trevor Story**, **Isaac Paredes**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Max Muncy**, **Moises Ballesteros**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Cole Young**, **Griffin Conine**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Travis Bazzana** to ensure that any late-day shifts in team rotations don't leave us exposed to defensive substitutions or pinch-hit scenarios that would unnecessarily burn games. **Vinnie Pasquantino** remains our primary anchor for stability, but with the current board reflecting a high density of sub-40 scores for our bench depth, we are strictly prioritizing efficiency over volume.",
